== ARCHIVE: definition-sensitivity control (step_e_kernel.py), cross pairing vs same-centre ==

Why this file exists. paper/software.md row S5 said, of these very numbers, that "no tracked
file in this repository holds these three values, so they are re-measurable but not checkable
against stored stdout". This run closes that: it is the same script, the same four systems and
the same grid levels, with its stdout now in a manifest-ed evidence directory.

Command: OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 PYTHONPATH=. python3 -u nlcsplit/step_e_kernel.py
Environment: WSL Linux, Python 3.10.12, PySCF 2.12.1 (~/.local), NumPy 2.2.6, basis 6-31g*,
xc wb97x_v. The script's own driver picks the levels - H2O/Ar/CH4 at level 1, benzene at
level 0 ("偏差不等网格收敛（step_b 已证），所以大分子用低 level 换可算性"), so the level is a
property of each row, not one number for the whole table.

Reading the table. "V0 现状" is the cross pairing g = w0(r_j)R^2 + kappa(r_i), which an
earlier implementation of ours used; "V2 同点" is the same-centre pairing the package now
defaults to. The column "与参照核差" is this package's explicit double sum minus PySCF's
native libdft VV10 kernel on one grid and one density, in kcal/mol, for the monomer.
V2 reproduces the native kernel to +0.0000 at the printed precision on all four systems,
which is the sense in which same-centre is "the convention that reproduces the native kernel".

What this run changed in the prose. Two claims did not survive it.

  1. paper/software.md gave the benzene figure as 0.914 kcal/mol and labelled all three
     values "at grid level 1". Measured here, benzene at level 0 is +0.9057, which is what
     paper/main.md already said; 0.914 is not reproduced by any tracked artefact, and the
     level-1 label was wrong for water and argon, which this script runs at level 1 alongside
     a level-0 benzene. software.md now carries 0.906 with per-value levels.
  2. Neither paper mentioned methane, which is in the same run: +0.1346 kcal/mol at level 1.

The two papers had been disagreeing with each other about one number (0.914 vs 0.906) while
both cited the same source row S5. That is what a missing carrier costs.

